ntation\" key containing Python source.\n\nWe must write the Python code as a string.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". The order of removed_node_ids is the removal order; retained_node_ids must be sorted.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nImplementation details: \n- Use case['nodes'] and case['required_symbols'].\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Build children lists: for each node, add to parent's children list. We'll create defaultdict(list).\n- Build set of required_symbols.\n- Create sorted list: sort node ids by depth desc, then id asc. Need depth from node dict.\n- removed_ids = set()\n- removal_order = []\n- For each node id in sorted list:\n    node = node_by_id[id]\n    if node['removable'] == False: continue\n    if node['parent'] is None: continue # root\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue\n    # check retained children: any child not in removed_ids\n    children = children_map.get(id, [])\n    if any(child_id not in removed_ids for child_id in children): continue\n    # check required symbols presence\n    # For each required symbol, need at least one node with that symbol not removed and not this node\n    ok = True\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        # find any node with that symbol that is not in removed_ids and not this node\n        # Since we don't have a precomputed mapping by symbol, we can scan all nodes each time (small)\n        # But we can precompute a map symbol->list of node ids.\n        # We'll precompute symbol_to_ids.\n    # If any missing, continue\n    # If ok, remove: add to removed_ids and removal_order.\n\nPrecompute symbol_to_ids: dict symbol -> list of node ids.\n\nThen for required symbol check:\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        # get list of ids for that symbol\n        ids_for_sym = symbol_to_ids.get(sym, [])\n        # check if any id in ids_for_sym is not removed and not current id\n        if not any( (nid not in removed_ids and nid != node_id) for nid in ids_for_sym ):\n            ok = False; break\n\nNow careful: Consider a required symbol that appears in root (symbol 'root'). root is not removed anyway (we skip root). So it's fine.\n\nNow after loop, retained_ids = set(node_by_id.keys()) - removed_ids.\nsorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids, key=str) (since they are strings anyway).\nReturn dict.\n\nWe must ensure output is JSON-serializable; lists of strings.\n\nNow double-check with first example: \nnodes: root(id root, sym root), n1 (s1), n2 (s2), n3 (s0), n4 (s1), n5 (s2), n6 (s0)\ndepth: root0, n1-1,n2-1,n3-1,n4-2,n5-2,n6-2.\nSorted descending depth: first depth2: n4, n5, n6 (ids sorted: n4,n5,n6). Process n4: removable true, not required sym (s1 is required, but n4 has s1? Actually required_symbols includes s1. So n4.symbol == 's1' which is in required_symbols. So we skip! This changes our earlier analysis. The rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So n4 cannot be removed because its symbol is s1 which is required. Even though there is another s1 (n1), the node itself has a required symbol, so cannot remove it. That makes sense because you cannot remove a node that produces a required symbol. So n4 is not removed.\nSimilarly n5: symbol s2 not required, but n5 is not removable (false). So skip.\nn6: symbol s0 not required, removable true. Check children: none. Check required symbols: for s1, we have n1 and n4 (n4 not removed yet). So s1 present. So n6 can be removed. Add removed_ids: n6.\nNext depth1: n1, n2, n3 (sorted: n1,n2,n3). n1: symbol s1 -> required, skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Check children: n4 (child) is not removed yet (n4 not in removed_ids because we skipped it). So n2 has a retained child (n4). Therefore cannot remove n2 because it has retained children. So skip n2. n3: symbol s0 not required, but removable false, skip.\nNext depth0: root: skip because parent None.\nSo only n6 removed. Retained: all except n6. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained